-- title:
--   Ideal cutoffs as predictions from earlier candidates
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix a candidate ledger at level $k$ with retained points $y_w$ and a common point $b$.
--
--   1. **Actual parameter.** For a node $v$ of level $k+1$, the restriction of the ledger coefficients to the candidates before $v$, used only after all weights are known.
--   2. **Ideal output.** The level-$(k+1)$ assignment
--   $$
--   H_v(w)=\begin{cases}y_w&w<v,\\ b&w\ge v,\end{cases}
--   $$
--   in which the current and all later children carry the common point.
--   3. **Identification.** The child averages of $H_v$ are the residual points of the actual parameter. Hence $\mathrm{ev}_{k+1}(H_v)$ is the evaluated prediction at that parameter.
